msgid "Data for your package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:3
msgid ""
"Here, you will learn about working with data for your scientific Python "
"package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:5
msgid "What you will learn"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:8
msgid "When and why you might need data,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:9
msgid "Where you can store your package's data,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:10
msgid ""
"How you can access the data both from within your package and by "
"downloading it as your package is used or as tests are run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:15
msgid "We adapted some of the material on this page from:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:17
msgid "<https://www.dampfkraft.com/code/distributing-large-files-with-pypi.html>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:18
msgid "<https://learn.scientific-python.org/development/patterns/data-files/>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:21
msgid "When and why you might need data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:23
msgid ""
"There are two cases when and why you might need data for maintaining and "
"using your package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:25
msgid ""
"Data for example usage: This is data that helps your users understand how"
" to use your package. This data is often used in documentation, "
"tutorials, and docstrings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:26
msgid ""
"Data for tests: This is data that helps you and your contributors make "
"sure your package is working as expected. This data is often used in unit"
" tests, integration tests, and end-to-end tests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:28
msgid "We'll talk through both use cases next."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:30
msgid "Data for example package usage and tutorials"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:32
msgid ""
"It's common for scientific Python packages to use example datasets for "
"tutorials and examples to help users understand how they can use a "
"library. Often, the package provides functionality to access this data, "
"Either by loading it from inside the package itself or by downloading it "
"from a remote host such as Figshare or another open repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:36
msgid ""
"This use case is so common that libraries exist to \"fetch\" data, like "
"[Pooch](https://www.fatiando.org/pooch/latest/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:39
msgid ""
"We will show you how to implement both, including data in your package or"
" downloading it below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:41
msgid "Examples of how scientific Python packages use data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:43
msgid ""
"**movingpandas** is a pyOpenSci-accepted package that uses data to "
"support some of its tutorials. [Here is an example of a tutorial that "
"shows how to use MovingPandas to process bird migration "
"data.](https://movingpandas.github.io/movingpandas-website/2-analysis-"
"examples/bird-migration.html)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:45
msgid ""
"[scikit-image](https://github.com/scikit-image/scikit-"
"image/tree/main/src/skimage/data)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:46
msgid ""
"[scikit-learn:](https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-"
"learn/tree/main/sklearn/datasets/data)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:48
msgid "Data for tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:50
msgid ""
"It is common to design your code and tests in such a way that you can "
"quickly test on data that you create yourself. You can store created data"
"  in any format. It can be simple (for example, a NumPy array of zeros) "
"or more complex ( a test suite that \"mocks\" data for a specific domain "
"or that mocks data returned through an API call)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:52
msgid ""
"Including created data in your package allows you to ensure the core "
"logic of your code works, without needing to download or use real data "
"(which is often large)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:54
msgid ""
"However, you should still make sure that your code runs properly using "
"real data formats and structures. This is why you should consider "
"including a small amount of real-world test data. This data should be "
"small enough that it can be included in your package without making the "
"package too large."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:56
msgid ""
"A good rule of thumb is to keep bundled data to a handful of small files "
"totalling no more than about 10 MB. Anything larger than that, you will "
"probably want to store online and download on demand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:60
msgid "Why you should download data: size limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:62
msgid ""
"As a general rule of thumb, you should download data from an online "
"source rather than including it in your package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:64
msgid "There are several reasons to store your data online:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:66
msgid ""
"There are limits on file and project sizes for forges, like GitHub and "
"GitLab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:67
msgid ""
"There are limits on file and project sizes for repositories and package "
"indexes like PyPI and Conda."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:68
msgid ""
"Downloading data as needed means that your package will be smaller and "
"faster to install."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:69
msgid ""
"Downloading data as needed means that you can update the data without "
"needing to re-release your package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:71
msgid ""
"It's essential to avoid placing unnecessary demands on our shared open "
"source infrastructure. These demands might be on the infrastructure "
"hosting the data (GitHub, PyPI) or the user downloading the package that "
"contains the data. Keep your example and test datasets as small as "
"possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:73
msgid "Forges (GitHub, GitLab, BitBucket, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:75
msgid ""
"Services like GitHub and GitLab, which host source code, have maximum "
"sizes for both files and projects. For GitHub, a single file cannot "
"exceed 100 MB. You might be surprised how quickly you can make a .csv "
"file this big!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:77
msgid ""
"You also want to avoid committing larger binary files (like images or "
"audio) to a version control system like git, because it is hard to go "
"back and remove them later. It can also slow down the speed at which you "
"can clone the project. More importantly, it slows down the speed at which"
" potential contributors can clone your project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:81
msgid "Data size and PyPI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:83
msgid ""
"The Python Package Index (PyPI) places a limit on the size of individual "
"files uploaded, where a \"file\" is either a sdist or a wheel--and also a"
" limit on the total size of the project (the sum of all the \"files\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:86
msgid ""
"The default limits are 100 MiB per file and 10 GB per project. See PyPI's"
" help page on [file size limits](https://pypi.org/help/#file-size-limit) "
"and [project size limits](https://pypi.org/help/#project-size-limit). "
"Files this large place a real strain on the resources supporting PyPI so "
"you should try your best to minimize the size of your package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:91
msgid "The pyOpenSci community is here to help you do just that!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:94
msgid ""
"You can request increases for both file size and project size (see "
"[here](https://pypi.org/help/#file-size-limit) and "
"[here](https://pypi.org/help/#project-size-limit)) But we strongly "
"suggest you read about other ways to store your data first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:100
msgid "Where to store your data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:102
msgid ""
"There are several options for storing your data. We will discuss the pros"
" and cons of each option below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:105
msgid "Within the repository"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:107
msgid ""
"There *are* cases where relatively small datasets can be safely included "
"in a package. If this data is used in package tutorials or examples and "
"the data are small, you can include it in your package. If the data are "
"included in your package, that means that it will be included in the "
"sdist and wheel and available to a user to run your tutorials or examples"
" after they install your package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:111
msgid ""
"If the data are meant to be used for tests, and you have a separate test "
"directory (as we suggest), then you can include the data in the tests "
"directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:113
msgid "There are pros and cons to including data in your package or repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:115
msgid "The pros include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:117
msgid "The data are easy to access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:118
msgid ""
"If the data size is small, it won't impact PyPI or a user downloading "
"your package from PyPI (for example, small snippets in text files, small "
"images, small CSV files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:120
msgid "The cons include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:122
msgid "Data can bloat your repository and make it hard to clone"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:123
msgid "Data can bloat your package and make it hard to install"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:124
msgid ""
"You may run into maximum file size limits on forges like GitHub and on "
"PyPI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:125
msgid ""
"If you update the data periodically, your repository bloat will increase "
"over time through the commit history."
msgstr ""

#: ../../tests/package-data.md:127
msgid ""
"You want to avoid adding these files to your version control history "
"(git) and draining the resources of PyPI."
msgstr ""

